00:01:05,680 --> 00:01:09,600
 There are a few high-level concepts we need to hit before we can even talk about material

17
00:01:09,600 --> 00:01:13,920
 properties and the first is the difference between a material property and a structural property.

18
00:01:13,920 --> 00:01:17,440
 The truth is that the performance of an engineering component, whether that's a mechanical

19
00:01:17,440 --> 00:01:22,320
 component or an electrical component or a medical component or whatever, is usually a function of both

20
00:01:22,320 --> 00:01:27,200
 the materials that it's made out of and the geometry of that component. A really obvious example

21
00:01:27,200 --> 00:01:32,160
 of this is weight. The weight of a component depends on both the density of the material that makes

22
00:01:32,160 --> 00:01:37,280
 it up, but also how much of that thing you have, how much space it takes up. The former, the density,

23
00:01:37,280 --> 00:01:41,840
 the thing that belongs just to the material, we call a material property. And the thing which

24
00:01:41,840 --> 00:01:45,680
 relates to both material and geometry, we usually call a structural property. In this case,

25
00:01:45,680 --> 00:01:50,800
 it would be mass. You might also hear these referred to as intensive or extensive properties or

26
00:01:50,800 --> 00:01:55,200
 intrinsic and extrinsic properties. In this course, we're largely going to be focused only on

27
00:01:55,200 --> 00:01:59,520
 material properties rather than structural properties, but of course, you should be aware that in

28
00:01:59,520 --> 00:02:03,920
 an engineering design where trade-offs exist, in other words, every engineering design ever,

29
00:02:03,920 --> 00:02:08,080
 there are probably ways that you can make up for the fact that a particular material has an

30
00:02:08,080 --> 00:02:13,440
 inferior property by some compromise to the geometry or vice versa. I should note that while we're

31
00:02:13,440 --> 00:02:17,840
 about to meet a bunch of material properties, it may seem like these things, these properties and

32
00:02:17,840 --> 00:02:23,200
 materials are fundamental laws of the universe. This isn't the case. A material properties could

33
00:02:23,200 --> 00:02:27,440
 often change as a function of environmental conditions, temperature and pressure, or even in the

34
00:02:27,440 --> 00:02:31,920
 direction that they're measured. Some materials, for example, are much stronger in one direction than

35
00:02:31,920 --> 00:02:36,720
 others. And in fact, this kind of variation of properties with the direction is so common that it has

36
00:02:36,720 --> 00:02:41,840
 a special name in material science. We call anisotropy. A material which has kind of the same

37
00:02:41,840 --> 00:02:45,920
 properties in every direction is said to be isotropic. A material which doesn't is said to be

38
00:02:45,920 --> 00:02:51,200
 anisotropic. You see this quite a lot in fibrous materials like woods where they're stronger

39
00:02:51,200 --> 00:02:55,360
 kind of along the grain than they are across it. And perhaps an example that's much more familiar

40
00:02:55,360 --> 00:03:01,040
 to you guys is in 3D prints where, again, they're much stronger in the direction of the grain of the

41
00:03:01,040 --> 00:03:05,440
 print than they are kind of between layers of the print. We're going to talk all about temperature

